  "slug": "phylogenomics-australian-76a6",
  "summary": "Four new preprints apply gene-dense, duplication-aware and whole-genome methods to resolve conflicting relationships across eukaryotes and deer.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of genome-scale data to resolve phylogenetic conflict, with each preprint addressing a different source of discordance. In Aizoaceae, phylotranscriptomic data resolve major relationships, but short internodes limit resolution within the rapid radiation. Reconciliation of 219 pre-LECA ancient gene duplication families is used to test candidate root positions of the eukaryote tree. The H2O workflow targets detection of large-scale gene family expansions, combining Ks distributions, tree-based duplication calls and paralogy-aware filtering. In Old World deer, whole-genome data attribute Miocene divergences mainly to incomplete lineage sorting, whereas Pliocene-Pleistocene splits show a gene flow contribution. What is new is the attention to data density, duplication history and the separation of sorting from introgression as distinct sources of discordance.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
